@subject acffa2
@chapter 14
@title The Trial Balance, Errors and Suspense Accounts
@lo E2, E3
@topic t1 | The trial balance | E2, E3
@topic t2 | Types of error | E2, E3
@topic t3 | Suspense accounts | E2, E3

=== Q001
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: What is a trial balance?
+ A list of all ledger account balances at a point in time, with total debits and total credits || A check on double entry.
- The statement of financial position || Not the same.
- A list of bank transactions || Not correct.
- A list of customers only || Not correct.
explain: Equal totals suggest, but do not prove, accuracy.
mistake: Thinking a balanced TB means no errors.
tip: All balances, two columns.
src: Study text ch14 - trial balance (concept basis; original question)

=== Q002
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Which error causes the trial balance NOT to balance?
+ A debit entry posted but the credit entry omitted || Single-sided.
- A transaction omitted completely || Both sides missing.
- An entry posted to the wrong customer's account || Commission.
- Debit and credit reversed || Reversal.
explain: Only errors making debits differ from credits show up.
mistake: Thinking omissions unbalance the TB.
tip: One-sided = imbalance.
src: Study text ch14 - errors (concept basis; original question)

=== Q003
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: The cost of a new van is debited to motor expenses. What type of error is this?
+ Error of principle || Wrong type of account (capital vs revenue).
- Error of commission || Same type, wrong account.
- Error of omission || Not omitted.
- Casting error || Not an addition error.
explain: The trial balance still balances.
mistake: Calling it commission.
tip: Wrong type = principle.
src: Study text ch14 - principle (concept basis; original question)

=== Q004
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Why is a suspense account opened?
+ To make the trial balance agree temporarily while the difference is investigated || Temporary.
- To hold cash || Not cash.
- To record capital || Not capital.
- To store fixed assets || Not assets.
explain: It must be cleared before the accounts are finalised.
mistake: Leaving it in the final accounts.
tip: Temporary holding account.
src: Study text ch14 - suspense (concept basis; original question)

=== Q005
type: num
level: F
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
q: A trial balance shows total debits of $145,600 and total credits of $148,300. What balance ($) is opened in the suspense account? (It is a debit balance.)
answer: 2700
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Suspense = difference between totals, on the short side
step: 148,300 - 145,600 = 2,700.
step: Debits are short, so suspense is a debit of $2,700.
check: 148300-145600
explain: The suspense balance goes on the side that is short.
mistake: Putting it on the larger side.
tip: Fill the short side.
src: Study text ch14 - opening suspense (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q006
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: A sale to customer A is posted to customer B's account. What type of error is this?
+ Error of commission || Right type, wrong account.
- Error of principle || Same type of account.
- Error of omission || It was recorded.
- Error of original entry || Amount is correct.
explain: The trial balance still balances.
mistake: Calling it principle.
tip: Same type, wrong account = commission.
src: Study text ch14 - commission (concept basis; original question)

=== Q007
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: An invoice for $45 is recorded as $54 in both the sales and receivables accounts. What type of error is this?
+ Error of original entry (transposition) || Wrong amount both sides.
- Single-sided entry || Both sides posted.
- Error of principle || Correct accounts.
- Error of omission || Recorded.
explain: Transposition differences are divisible by 9.
mistake: Expecting a suspense entry.
tip: Same wrong amount both sides = balances.
src: Study text ch14 - original entry (concept basis; original question)

=== Q008
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Which account normally has a DEBIT balance in the trial balance?
+ Rent expense || Expense.
- Sales revenue || Credit.
- Capital || Credit.
- Trade payables || Credit.
explain: Debits: assets, expenses, drawings.
mistake: Placing income as a debit.
tip: DEAD CLIC.
src: Study text ch14 - balances (concept basis; original question)

=== Q009
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Which errors are corrected WITHOUT using the suspense account?
+ Errors that did not cause the trial balance to disagree, such as omission or principle || Both sides were posted.
- Single-sided entries || Use suspense.
- Casting errors in one account || Use suspense.
- Unequal debit and credit amounts || Use suspense.
explain: Only imbalance errors involve suspense.
mistake: Using suspense for every correction.
tip: Balanced error = no suspense.
src: Study text ch14 - corrections (concept basis; original question)

=== Q010
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: A difference of $360 in the trial balance may be caused by which error?
+ A transposition in one account, as the difference is divisible by 9 || Clue.
- An omitted transaction || No difference.
- A reversal of entries || No difference.
- An error of commission || No difference.
explain: Transposed digits create differences divisible by 9.
mistake: Ignoring the 9 test.
tip: Divisible by 9 = check transpositions.
src: Study text ch14 - transposition (concept basis; original question)

=== Q011
type: mcq
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
q: A credit sale of $2,400 was credited to sales but not debited to receivables. What is the correcting journal?
+ Dr receivables $2,400; Cr suspense $2,400 || Complete the missing side.
- Dr suspense $2,400; Cr sales $2,400 || Sales is already correct.
- Dr receivables $2,400; Cr sales $2,400 || Double counts sales.
- Dr sales $2,400; Cr receivables $2,400 || Reverses the sale.
formula: Correction = what should have been - what was done; other side to suspense
step: Sales was credited correctly; receivables debit missing.
step: Dr receivables 2,400; Cr suspense 2,400.
check: 2400
explain: Profit is not affected.
mistake: Crediting sales again.
tip: Fix the missing side via suspense.
src: Study text ch14 - single-sided (concept basis; original question)

=== Q012
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
q: A machine costing $5,000 was debited to repairs. Ignoring depreciation, by how much ($) does profit change when the error is corrected?
answer: 5000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Profit change = expense removed
step: Correction: Dr machinery 5,000; Cr repairs 5,000.
step: Expenses fall by 5,000, so profit rises by $5,000.
check: 5000
explain: No suspense entry is needed.
mistake: Using suspense.
tip: Capital wrongly expensed = profit up on correction.
src: Study text ch14 - principle (concept basis; original question)

=== Q013
type: mr
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
select: 2
q: Which TWO errors do NOT cause the trial balance to disagree?
+ Debit and credit entries reversed || Reversal.
+ Two errors that exactly cancel each other || Compensating.
- Only the debit side of an entry posted || Single-sided.
- The sales account added up incorrectly || Casting error.
explain: Errors of omission, commission, principle and original entry also balance.
mistake: Thinking reversals unbalance the TB.
tip: Equal debits and credits still.
src: Study text ch14 - errors (concept basis; original question)

=== Q014
type: match
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Match each error with its type.
pair: Transaction left out completely => Omission
pair: Posted to the wrong account of the same type => Commission
pair: Capital item posted as an expense => Principle
pair: Same wrong amount used on both sides => Original entry
explain: None of these unbalances the TB.
mistake: Mixing commission and principle.
tip: Type of account decides.
src: Study text ch14 - error types (concept basis; original question)

=== Q015
type: mcq
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
q: The sales account was undercast (added up short) by $1,000. What is the correcting journal?
+ Dr suspense $1,000; Cr sales $1,000 || Sales must increase.
- Dr sales $1,000; Cr suspense $1,000 || Reduces sales.
- Dr receivables $1,000; Cr sales $1,000 || Receivables are correct.
- No journal - casting errors balance || They unbalance the TB.
formula: Only one account is wrong; the other side goes to suspense
step: Sales should be 1,000 higher (credit).
step: Dr suspense 1,000; Cr sales 1,000.
check: 1000
explain: Profit rises by $1,000.
mistake: Debiting sales.
tip: Undercast credit = credit it.
src: Study text ch14 - casting error (concept basis; original question)

=== Q016
type: order
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Put the steps for correcting an error in order.
item: Identify what entry was actually made
item: Identify what entry should have been made
item: Prepare a journal for the difference
item: Post any one-sided difference to the suspense account
explain: Then check whether profit is affected.
mistake: Guessing the journal.
tip: Was, should be, difference.
src: Study text ch14 - correction method (concept basis; original question)

=== Q017
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: If the CREDIT side of the trial balance is short, what balance does the suspense account have?
+ A credit balance || It fills the short side.
- A debit balance || That is when debits are short.
- No balance || There is a difference.
- It depends on profit || Not related.
explain: The suspense balance makes the TB agree temporarily.
mistake: Putting it on the longer side.
tip: Short side.
src: Study text ch14 - suspense side (concept basis; original question)

=== Q018
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: A payment of $1,730 was recorded in both accounts as $1,370. By how much ($) are both accounts understated?
answer: 360
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Error = correct amount - recorded amount
step: 1,730 - 1,370.
step: = $360 (divisible by 9).
check: 1730-1370
explain: This is an error of original entry; no suspense.
mistake: Expecting a TB difference.
tip: Both sides wrong equally.
src: Study text ch14 - transposition (concept basis; original question)

=== Q019
type: mcq
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
q: A $500 rent payment was credited to bank but not debited to rent. What is the correcting journal and the effect on profit?
+ Dr rent $500; Cr suspense $500; profit falls by $500 || Expense was missing.
- Dr suspense $500; Cr rent $500; profit rises || Reversed.
- Dr rent $500; Cr bank $500; profit falls || Bank is already correct.
- No journal; no effect || A correction is needed.
formula: Complete the missing debit via suspense
step: Bank credit is correct; rent debit is missing.
step: Dr rent 500; Cr suspense 500; expenses up.
check: 500
explain: Recording the missing expense reduces profit.
mistake: Crediting bank again.
tip: Missing expense = profit down.
src: Study text ch14 - single-sided (concept basis; original question)

=== Q020
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Which statement about a balancing trial balance is correct?
+ It shows debits equal credits, but errors such as omissions may still exist || Limited check.
- It proves the accounts are correct || It does not.
- It proves there is no fraud || It does not.
- It means no suspense account was ever needed || Not necessarily.
explain: Several error types do not affect the totals.
mistake: Over-relying on the TB.
tip: Balanced ≠ correct.
src: Study text ch14 - limitations (concept basis; original question)

=== Q021
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Electricity of $300 is debited to bank and credited to electricity. What type of error is this?
+ Reversal of entries || Debit and credit swapped.
- Error of omission || Recorded.
- Error of principle || Correct accounts.
- Single-sided entry || Both sides posted.
explain: Correcting requires twice the amount: Dr electricity 600, Cr bank 600.
mistake: Correcting by the original amount only.
tip: Reversal = double the correction.
src: Study text ch14 - reversal (concept basis; original question)

=== Q022
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: An electricity payment of $300 was recorded as Dr bank, Cr electricity. What amount ($) is needed in the correcting journal (Dr electricity, Cr bank)?
answer: 600
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Correction for a reversal = 2 x amount
step: Remove the wrong entry (300) and post the right one (300).
step: Journal = $600.
check: 300*2
explain: Profit falls by $600 when corrected.
mistake: Using $300.
tip: Undo and redo.
src: Study text ch14 - reversal correction (concept basis; original question)

=== Q023
type: mcq
level: S
cat: trap
topic: t3
status: review
q: A trainee corrects an error of principle (a van debited to motor expenses) by "Dr vans, Cr suspense". What is wrong?
+ Both sides were originally posted, so the correction is Dr vans, Cr motor expenses - suspense is not involved || No TB imbalance.
- Nothing is wrong || Suspense is wrong.
- The correction should be Dr suspense, Cr vans || Not correct.
- No correction is needed || One is needed.
explain: Using suspense would create a new imbalance.
mistake: Using suspense for balanced errors.
tip: Balanced error = no suspense.
src: Study text ch14 - principle correction (concept basis; original question)

=== Q024
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: What is a compensating error?
+ Two separate errors of equal amount that cancel out in the trial balance || Hidden.
- One error that corrects itself || Not the meaning.
- An error that the bank makes || Not the meaning.
- An error of principle || Different type.
explain: The TB balances despite two mistakes.
mistake: Thinking they cause imbalance.
tip: Two wrongs hide each other.
src: Study text ch14 - compensating (concept basis; original question)

=== Q025
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: A suspense account was opened with a debit balance of $1,260. Errors found: a $900 rent payment was credited to bank but not debited to rent; discount allowed of $360 was omitted from the discount allowed account but recorded in receivables.
q: What is the suspense balance ($) after correcting both errors?
answer: 0
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Suspense = opening balance - corrections
step: Dr rent 900, Cr suspense 900; Dr discount allowed 360, Cr suspense 360.
step: 1,260 - 900 - 360 = 0.
check: 1260-900-360
explain: Profit falls by $1,260 after the corrections.
mistake: Leaving a balance.
tip: Corrections should clear suspense.
src: Study text ch14 - clearing suspense (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q026
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Can a suspense account balance appear in the final published financial statements?
+ No - it must be investigated and cleared first || Temporary only.
- Yes, as a current asset || Not allowed.
- Yes, as equity || Not allowed.
- Yes, if it is small || It should be cleared.
explain: All errors should be corrected before finalising.
mistake: Leaving suspense balances.
tip: Clear before finalising.
src: Study text ch14 - suspense (concept basis; original question)

=== Q027
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
q: The discount received account was debited with $250 instead of being credited. Receivables and payables were posted correctly. What is the correcting entry amount ($) to discount received (credit)?
answer: 500
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Correction = twice the amount (remove wrong debit, add correct credit)
step: Wrong debit 250 removed: Cr 250; correct credit 250 added: Cr 250.
step: Cr discount received 500; Dr suspense 500.
check: 250*2
explain: The TB was out by $500, so suspense is involved.
mistake: Correcting by $250.
tip: Wrong side in one account = 2x.
src: Study text ch14 - wrong side (concept basis; original question)

=== Q028
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: A purchase invoice is never entered in the books. What type of error is this, and does the TB balance?
+ Error of omission; the TB still balances || Both sides missing.
- Single-sided error; the TB does not balance || Neither side posted.
- Error of principle; TB does not balance || Not principle.
- Casting error; TB balances || Not casting.
explain: Supplier statement reconciliations can reveal omissions.
mistake: Expecting a TB difference.
tip: Nothing posted = balanced.
src: Study text ch14 - omission (concept basis; original question)

=== Q029
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Correction journals sometimes change profit. Which correction INCREASES profit?
+ Moving a capital item wrongly charged to expenses into non-current assets || Expense removed.
- Recording an omitted expense || Decreases profit.
- Correcting an overcast sales account || Decreases revenue.
- Moving an expense between two expense accounts || No change.
explain: Prepare a statement of adjustments to profit for several corrections.
mistake: Assuming corrections never affect profit.
tip: Remove expense = profit up.
src: Study text ch14 - profit effect (concept basis; original question)

=== Q030
type: mcq
level: S
cat: trap
topic: t1
status: review
q: A trainee says, "Our trial balance agrees, so our accounts have no errors." Which error could still exist?
+ An invoice posted to the wrong supplier's account || Commission - balances.
- A debit posted without a credit || Would unbalance.
- An account added up wrongly || Would unbalance.
- Unequal debit and credit amounts || Would unbalance.
explain: Many errors leave the TB in balance.
mistake: Over-trusting the TB.
tip: Balanced ≠ correct.
src: Study text ch14 - limits (concept basis; original question)

=== Q031
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Draft profit is $48,000. Errors found: a machine costing $5,000 was debited to repairs (depreciation at 10% a year, full year); an accrual of $800 for electricity was omitted; the sales account was undercast by $1,000.
q: What is the corrected profit ($)?
answer: 52700
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Corrected profit = draft + adjustments
step: Machine: +5,000 - depreciation 500 = +4,500; accrual: -800.
step: Sales undercast: +1,000; corrected = 48,000 + 4,500 - 800 + 1,000 = $52,700.
check: 48000+5000-500-800+1000
explain: Each correction is considered separately.
mistake: Forgetting the new depreciation.
tip: Capitalised assets need depreciation.
src: Study text ch14 - profit adjustments (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q032
type: mcq
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
q: Cash sales of $640 were recorded correctly in the bank account but credited to the sales account as $460. What is the correcting journal?
+ Dr suspense $180; Cr sales $180 || Sales understated by 180.
- Dr sales $180; Cr suspense $180 || Reduces sales.
- Dr bank $180; Cr sales $180 || Bank is correct.
- Dr suspense $640; Cr sales $640 || Overcorrects.
formula: Difference = 640 - 460
step: Sales is short by 180 on the credit side.
step: Dr suspense 180; Cr sales 180.
check: 640-460
explain: Profit rises by $180.
mistake: Debiting bank.
tip: Only the wrong account changes.
src: Study text ch14 - unequal entries (concept basis; original question)

=== Q033
type: mr
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
select: 2
q: Which TWO errors require a suspense account entry when corrected?
+ The total of the purchases day book was posted to purchases but not to payables || One side missing.
+ A $95 payment was debited to wages as $59 but credited to bank as $95 || Unequal amounts.
- A sale was posted to the wrong customer || Commission.
- Rent paid was debited to rates || Commission.
explain: Only errors that unbalanced the TB use suspense.
mistake: Using suspense for commission errors.
tip: Unequal = suspense.
src: Study text ch14 - suspense (concept basis; original question)

=== Q034
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: A suspense account has a CREDIT balance of $1,450. Errors: (1) the sales account was undercast by $1,000; (2) a $450 payment to a supplier was debited to payables but not credited to bank.
q: After both errors are corrected (clearing the suspense account), by how much ($) does profit increase?
answer: 1000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Profit effect = corrections to income and expense accounts only
step: (1) Dr suspense 1,000; Cr sales 1,000 - sales rises by 1,000.
step: (2) Dr suspense 450; Cr bank 450 - no profit effect; suspense = 1,450 - 1,000 - 450 = 0.
check: 1000
explain: Only the sales correction touches profit or loss.
mistake: Treating the bank correction as a profit item.
tip: SOFP corrections don't change profit.
src: Study text ch14 - suspense clearing (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q035
type: mcq
level: C
cat: trap
topic: t3
status: review
q: A trainee opens a suspense account with a debit balance when the trial balance credits exceed debits. Is this correct?
+ Yes - the debit side is short, so the suspense balance is a debit || Fill the short side.
- No - it should be a credit || The debit side is short.
- No - suspense accounts are never debits || They can be.
- It depends on profit || Not related.
explain: Credits exceeding debits means debits are short.
mistake: Reading the difference the wrong way.
tip: Which side is smaller?
src: Study text ch14 - suspense side (concept basis; original question)

=== Q036
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Draft profit $62,500. Corrections: (1) rent paid of $1,200 recorded only in bank; (2) drawings of $800 debited to wages; (3) closing inventory overstated by $1,500.
q: What is the corrected profit ($)?
answer: 60600
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Corrected profit = draft ± each adjustment
step: (1) Rent expense -1,200; (2) remove wages expense +800 (drawings are not an expense).
step: (3) Inventory overstated -1,500; corrected = 62,500 - 1,200 + 800 - 1,500 = $60,600.
check: 62500-1200+800-1500
explain: Only (1) involves suspense.
mistake: Treating drawings as an expense.
tip: Analyse each error's P/L effect.
src: Study text ch14 - profit statement (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q037
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Insurance paid of $600 was debited to the rates account. What type of error is this and does profit change on correction?
+ Error of commission; profit is unchanged, as both are expenses || Moves between expenses.
- Error of principle; profit changes || Same type of account.
- Omission; profit changes || It was recorded.
- Single-sided; suspense needed || Both sides posted.
explain: Correction: Dr insurance, Cr rates.
mistake: Thinking profit changes.
tip: Expense to expense = no profit effect.
src: Study text ch14 - commission (concept basis; original question)

=== Q038
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
q: A trial balance has total debits of $286,450. The suspense account has a CREDIT balance of $1,750. What are total credits ($) before the suspense account is added?
answer: 284700
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Credits = debits - credit suspense
step: A credit suspense balance fills a credit shortfall.
step: Credits = 286,450 - 1,750 = $284,700.
check: 286450-1750
explain: After adding suspense, both sides total 286,450.
mistake: Adding the suspense to debits.
tip: Suspense fills the short side.
src: Study text ch14 - TB totals (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q039
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t3
status: review
q: After clearing all known errors, the suspense account still has a small balance. What should the accountant do?
+ Continue investigating; there are further errors to find || Suspense must be nil.
- Write the balance off to profit without explanation || Not appropriate.
- Show it as a current asset || Not allowed.
- Add it to capital || Not appropriate.
explain: Unexplained balances can hide larger errors.
mistake: Writing off unexplained differences.
tip: Keep looking.
src: Study text ch14 - investigation (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q040
type: match
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Match each error with its correcting journal.
pair: Cash sale $200 credited to sales, not debited to bank => Dr bank 200, Cr suspense 200
pair: Repairs $300 debited to equipment => Dr repairs 300, Cr equipment 300
pair: Rent $150 debited to rates => Dr rent 150, Cr rates 150
pair: Wages account overcast by $90 => Dr suspense 90, Cr wages 90
explain: Only one-sided or casting errors use suspense.
mistake: Using suspense for commission or principle.
tip: Was it balanced?
src: Study text ch14 - journals (concept basis; original question)

=== Q041
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
q: The total of the sales day book, $12,600, was posted to receivables but credited to sales as $12,060. What is the effect ($) on profit when corrected?
answer: 540
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Effect = correct sales - recorded sales
step: 12,600 - 12,060 = 540.
step: Dr suspense 540; Cr sales 540; profit up $540.
check: 12600-12060
explain: Divisible by 9 - a transposition in one account.
mistake: Adjusting receivables.
tip: Fix the wrong side only.
src: Study text ch14 - transposition (concept basis; original question)

=== Q042
type: mcq
level: C
cat: trap
topic: t2
status: review
q: A trainee says a casting error in the wages account "doesn't matter because the trial balance will show it". Why is this a weak view?
+ The TB shows a difference but not where it is; the error still misstates profit until corrected || Detection is not correction.
- The trainee is right; no action is needed || Correction is needed.
- Casting errors never affect profit || They can.
- The bank will correct it || Not the bank's role.
explain: Investigate and correct through suspense.
mistake: Assuming detection fixes errors.
tip: Find and fix.
src: Study text ch14 - casting (concept basis; original question)

=== Q043
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: The suspense account was opened with a debit balance of $730. Errors: (1) a cheque for $280 paid to a supplier was credited to bank but not debited to payables; (2) the discount allowed account was undercast by $90; (3) interest received of $360 was credited to interest received but not debited to bank.
q: What is the suspense balance ($) after correcting these three errors? (Enter 0 if cleared.)
answer: 0
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Suspense = opening debit - corrections crediting suspense
step: (1) Dr payables 280, Cr suspense 280; (3) Dr bank 360, Cr suspense 360.
step: (2) Dr discount allowed 90, Cr suspense 90; balance = 730 - 280 - 360 - 90 = 0.
check: 730-280-360-90
explain: All three errors left the debit side short; profit falls by $90 (discount allowed).
mistake: Thinking the interest correction affects profit.
tip: Each error explains part of the difference.
src: Study text ch14 - suspense (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q044
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Why might a business prepare a "statement of adjustments to profit" after correcting errors?
+ To show how the draft profit changes to the corrected profit, error by error || Transparent reconciliation.
- To calculate tax only || Not the purpose.
- To clear the suspense account automatically || Journals do that.
- To replace the trial balance || Not the purpose.
explain: Only corrections affecting income or expenses change profit.
mistake: Including SOFP-only corrections as profit changes.
tip: List each P/L effect.
src: Study text ch14 - adjustments to profit (concept basis; original question)

=== Q045
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t2
status: review
q: A purchase invoice for $2,340 was recorded in both purchases and payables as $3,240. By how much ($) is profit understated?
answer: 900
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Overstated expense = recorded - correct
step: 3,240 - 2,340 = 900.
step: Purchases overstated by 900, so profit understated by $900.
check: 3240-2340
explain: Original entry error; no suspense.
mistake: Using suspense.
tip: Both sides wrong = no suspense.
src: Study text ch14 - original entry (concept basis; original question)

=== Q046
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t2
status: review
q: A clerk posts a customer receipt of $1,000 to the correct customer's account but debits the cash account with $100. What kind of error is this?
+ Unequal entries - the trial balance will not balance || Different amounts.
- Commission || Accounts are correct.
- Omission || Both sides posted.
- Compensating || Only one error.
explain: Correction: Dr cash 900, Cr suspense 900.
mistake: Missing the imbalance.
tip: Different amounts = suspense.
src: Study text ch14 - unequal entries (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q047
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: When an error is corrected through the suspense account, what happens to the trial balance?
+ It still balances, and the suspense balance is reduced by the correction || Moves toward nil.
- It stops balancing || Corrections keep it balanced.
- The suspense account doubles || Not correct.
- Nothing changes || Suspense reduces.
explain: When all errors are fixed, suspense is nil.
mistake: Expecting imbalance after corrections.
tip: Corrections shrink suspense.
src: Study text ch14 - process (concept basis; original question)

=== Q048
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t3
status: review
scenario: Draft profit $34,200. Corrections: (1) sales returns of $650 were credited to sales returns instead of debited (receivables correctly credited); (2) a payment for office equipment of $2,000 was debited to stationery.
q: What is the corrected profit ($)?
answer: 34900
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Corrected = draft - returns correction + equipment correction
step: (1) Returns should be debit 650 but were credited 650: correction Dr 1,300, reducing profit by 1,300.
step: (2) Remove stationery expense +2,000; corrected = 34,200 - 1,300 + 2,000 = $34,900.
check: 34200-1300+2000
explain: Wrong-side errors need double the amount.
mistake: Correcting returns by 650.
tip: Wrong side = double.
src: Study text ch14 - profit adjustments (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q049
type: mcq
level: F
cat: revision
topic: t2
status: review
important: 1
q: Which errors leave the trial balance in balance?
+ Omission, commission, principle, original entry, reversal and compensating errors || Six balanced types.
- Single-sided and casting errors || These unbalance it.
- Only casting errors || Unbalance.
- No errors ever balance || Several do.
explain: Only imbalance errors use the suspense account.
mistake: Thinking all errors show up.
tip: Learn the six.
src: Study text ch14 - errors (concept basis; original question)

=== Q050
type: mcq
level: S
cat: revision
topic: t3
status: review
important: 1
q: Which statement about suspense accounts is correct?
+ A suspense account is temporary and is cleared by correcting the errors that caused the TB difference || Core rule.
- It is a permanent asset account || Temporary.
- It is used for all error corrections || Only imbalance errors.
- Its balance is always shown in the SOFP || Must be cleared.
explain: The balance sits on the TB's short side.
mistake: Using it for balanced errors.
tip: Temporary; clear it.
src: Study text ch14 - suspense (concept basis; original question)
